intuitive way to present order picking information [7, 8]. In pick-by-light approaches, lamps are installed with each of the picking items on the shelves instead of pick-by-paper. When a new order is given, the lamp corresponding to the target item is turned on to illustrate the location to pick. Nevertheless, multiple workers’ picking performance on the same shelf is not supported at the same time by such a system. While in the pick-by-voice system, workers wear a headset and receive auditory instructions about the next item to be collected. During the manual order picking process, considerable mental efforts are required since pickers must remember what has been told, and they have to associate the auditory instruction with the geometrical layout of the warehouse shelf [9].
Augmented reality (AR) is an innovative technology that supports the human visual sense, and the time for information guidance can be reduced drastically by data visualization in the user’s field of view [10, 11]. In warehouse manual order picking, AR can convey the logical picking information into visual guidelines, and it is identified that AR is competitive both in terms of time efficiency, and picking accuracy [12]. Nevertheless, to enable the AR application for manual order picking, robust tracking for the wearable AR system is essential in actual environments, especially in industrial workplaces. Schwerdtfeger et al. [9] proposed an outside-in tracking system with some spherical reflective markers fixed on the head-mounted display (HMD); given multi-infrared cameras arranged fixed within the workplace, robust navigation for the subsequent AR-assist order picking is achieved. Hanson et al. [13] performed the pick-by-AR application with Microsoft Hololens (www.microsoft.com), which equipped with a selfcontained tracking module to enable the AR performance for manual order picking instruction. Nevertheless, it is prone to tracking lost in the industrial scene with dynamic and featureless environments, and it is hard to relate the superimposed visual guidance to the physical warehouse floor. Instead of utilizing existed tracking modules for AR, Tumler et al. [14] related the compartment in the shelf with a specified fiducial marker, enabling AR-assisted order picking by identifying the corresponding marker. Nevertheless, due to the lack of global mapping information about fiducial markers within the warehouse floor, this individual marker-based pick-byAR method can only work when the target marker is within the field of view of the camera, limiting the picker to some certain localizations and orientations for AR instructions. In summary, the adaptability and stability of the current methods are difficult to come into use for a scalable and long-term pick-by-AR application, and a systematic pick-by-AR solution related to the real warehouse workplace is still unavailable and worthy of further study.
Thus, different from the existing pick-by-AR methods, the purpose of the paper is to establish adaptability and scalable wearable AR assistance system for order picking related to the

actual warehouse workplace. The contributions of this paper are the following:
1. To enable a long-term AR-assisted manual order picking system, the easy-to-deploy marker-based global map related to the warehouse shelves is proposed and the physical layout of the related order picking items is determined automatically.
2. Given the recovered spatial localization of multi-markers in the workplace, accurate and long-term navigation for pick-by-AR is available, allowing the operator to move freely and target-oriented by the AR assistance in the warehouse floor without limiting to some certain locations.
3. By integrating the proposed method to a lightweight AR device, a systematic wearable AR solution for manual order picking is proposed and evaluated in the actual automobile assembly line.
This paper is structured as follows: Section 2 reviews the related works. Section 3 describes the global map establishment with fiducial markers for the wearable AR picking system. In Section 4, the tracking performance evaluation of the proposed method and experiments about the pick-by-AR system are carried out in the automobile assembly line. In Section 5, results about the AR-assisted manual picking system are discussed. Finally, the conclusion and future work of the research are presented.
2 Literature review
2.1 Order picking
In order picking tasks, lots of items from assortments are collected by workers, and then, they are delivered to the next station in a designed flow process [15]. Such a scenario occurs thousands of times in an assembly line. This means that the research of manual order picking is of significance to improve current productivity. Traditionally, order picking is accomplished by providing workers with a paper list, illustrating their positions in the warehouse shelf and the number of items to be carried. To increase picking efficiency and reduce picking errors, other methods are applied to perform order picking assistance, such as pick-by-light, pick-by-voice, and pick-byAR [16].
Pick-by-paper has been applied for a long time in order picking, and it entailed a paper list on the clipboard mounted on a picking cart. Then, operators perform order picking operations according to a predefined procedure. The layout of the paper list is critical for order picking, including the sequential logic between different order lines and the spatial guidance associated with storage locations [17, 18], which can be

