designed according to the industry standards studied [8]. A joint confirmation for the placement of components was performed by highlighting the order line with a pen tied to the clipboard when it had been accomplished. Columns indicated storage location, part description, and a checkbox to confirm the order line as completed. The pick-by-paper method has the benefit of being flexible and requiring small investment costs. Nevertheless, these list-based order picking processes force the picker to perform each step within two stages: read and interpret the list’s illustrations and then take a component from one area to another. The text-only lists are difficult to read and interpret when the product numbers are too long. Besides, workers have the paper list in one hand while reaching for the target part, limiting the number of parts they can process at the same time, and it is prone to a high error rate [19].
Pick-by-light refers to an information system where a light indicates the location for the next picking activity [6] and it is often combined with a small display and push buttons. Multiple variants of pick confirmation exist for pick-by-light, of which the most common is to press the button to turn off the light. When pickers traverse aisles, the displays illustrated on the pick bins show the quantity to be picked. Usually, one light-up button positioned above each box with components in storage racks, illuminating the next picking location. The picked components were confirmed by triggering the lighted button. And the wireless light-up units with displays and confirmation buttons were located above the kits to inform the picker in which kits to place picked components and to confirm placement. However, in the pick-by-light process, pickers dislike the buttons on pick bins due to they do not always detect the reach correctly [16].
Pick-by-voice applied voice communication to guide pickers on the warehouse floor. In previous studies, these methods can eliminate picking errors and achieve a greater picking quality [20]. A headset linked via Bluetooth to a base unit is carried in the picker’s pocket which can transmit picking information from a nearby host computer. Firstly, a designation indicating the storage location to pick from is received by the picker. Then, the location was confirmed by returning a location-specific code. This process was performed repeatedly until all components had been placed in target localizations. The voice picking system allows workers to concentrate on the picking target and have both hands free to execute order picking performance. However, noisy industrial environments limit the voice-directed order picking performance.
Lastly, pick-by-AR is an innovative alternative for order picking, referring to a picking information system that uses HMD and visualizations to indicate picking operations [21]. Various pick-by-AR solutions have been studied in warehouse order picking [12, 13], and they presented several findings regarding what type of AR visualizations are suitable to guide a picker. To enable the pick-by-AR in the warehouse

floor, accurate and robust tracking performance is essential. Thus, the storage location to pick was indicated as a highlight area on the shelf along with the quantity to be picked. When contrasted with other picking information systems, these pickby-AR studies have shown promising potential both in terms of quality and efficiency [21]. However, existed pick-by-AR researches most pay attention to the comparisons of different assisted AR tools on the shelf, such as the Google Glass and Microsoft Hololens, lacking systematic and scalable solutions to order picking applications in the actual workplace. In addition, the robust and global navigation for pick-by-AR in the warehouse floor is still unaddressed effectively, and which is also the purpose of the study in this paper.
2.2 Navigation in pick-by-AR
AR is an innovative human-computer interaction technology that superimposes the visual model in a context-sensitive way, and it is appropriate for a specific manual task relative to the user’s physical location [22]. The real use of AR in industrial contexts is still in infancy, such as in the fields of design, production, and serving [11, 23]. In order picking, the operator receives picking and placing information via the graphical interface, and it indicates that AR has the potential to improve the current order picking performance.
The main task of AR in order picking is to navigate the worker intuitively on the warehouse floor while doing manual picking actions. To enable appropriate visual guidance for manual order picking related to the actual workplace, an accurate navigation capacity, determining the localization and orientation of the AR system relative to the warehouse shelf, is essential for wearable AR applications in manual order picking [24] and other industrial environments [25].
Head-up displays like Google Glass were used to support manual order picking, and the visual guidance for picking action displayed on the screen can result in fewer picking errors [16, 21]. However, due to the lack of accurate navigation capacity of Google Glass on the warehouse floor, the localization and orientation of the AR device in the warehouse floor cannot be precepted. Thus, it is impossible to superimpose the visual picking instruction on the actual warehouse shelves reasonably. High-intensive mental efforts for pickers are still needed to decide where to relate the next picking action with the warehouse floor according to the visual instruction on head-up displays. Similarly, Hanson et al. [13] used Microsoft Hololens as a head-up display to convey picking information into visual guidance for manual order picking, and they also did not establish an accurate spatial correspondence between the visual guidance and the physical warehouse floor. Although Microsoft Hololens qualified a powerful self-contained tracking capacity in an unknown environment, it is prone to drift and tracking lost within the textureless industrial environment. Besides, the weight of Microsoft

