Hololens is about 0.5 kg, imposing a challenge for a long-term order picking assistance. Given the diversity of device characteristics with smart glasses in industrial applications, a simulation-based approach was presented to ground designed decisions toward AR-based solutions in order picking [26]. Google Glass and Microsoft Hololens were chosen as the two AR devices in their research, and the results revealed that AR-assisted method can increase task performance while reducing task load in manual order picking. However, these “warehouse shelf-isolated” pick-by-AR methods also ignored the spatial correspondence between the augmented display in HMD with the actual warehouse shelf distribution, and the picker still needs to make considerable mental efforts to find the target component within the shelf according to the visual instruction. In addition, taken into consideration the limited field of view of current AR devices (such as Google Glass or Microsoft Hololens), the augmented instruction is only visible partially on the display. Usually, visual instruction is outside the field of view when workers are looking at the wrong direction, resulting in an unfriendly pick-by-AR experience.
Different from the “warehouse shelf-isolated” pick-by-AR applications, the marker-based navigation system is still a frequently adopted solution in the manufacturing industry because of its easy-to-use and low-cost [11, 27]. In addition, given the pose estimation from the fiducial marker identification, the marker-based pick-by-AR can relate the visual instruction with the actual physical warehouse floor. The augmented reality instructions are superimposed exactly on the actual workplace intuitively, and the worker can be led by the AR system step by step [28], alleviating the mental efforts to find the exact localization of the target bins for the worker. Reif et al. [24] proposed an outside-in tracking system where some spherical reflective markers are mounted on the HMD, and the localization and orientation of the AR device can be perceived by arranging some infrared cameras within the warehouse floor. Thus, the superimposed display showed pickers the next stock location and the picking information. Nevertheless, this navigation method is not flexible to the warehouse layout change, and occlusions, resulting in the failure of AR instructions, are prone to occur when the picker performs order picking actions in actual workplaces.
To improve the adaptability of the marker-based system, Tumler [14] placed the marker on shelf bin, and with the help of the individual fiducial marker for AR navigation, a systematic mental effort analysis with the usage of AR technology was performed. Fiducial marker-based tracking method for AR order picking is proposed by sticking markers on the warehouse shelf [29]. When performing the manual picking actions, the camera equipped in the AR system can capture the fiducial marker in the real scene. Based on the commercial Metaio for marker-based AR tracking, the localization and orientation of the wearable AR device are determined when the target marker is located within the field of view; thus, the

visual instruction for order picking is available directly. Nevertheless, due to lack of the entire spatial map about all the markers in the warehouse floor, these marker-based navigation methods for pick-by-AR are based on the individual marker alone, which can only work only if the target marker can be seen and identified accurately, lacking the ability to make full use of other markers in the warehouse floor. In addition, before the pick-by-AR process with this markerbased navigation, the prior knowledge about the marker ID attached on the shelf should be given manually; it is also a time-consuming and error-prone task. If the fiducial marker on the shelf fell or became dirty during the order picking performance, this individual marker-based pick-by-AR system for visual guidance would fail to work. Inspired by these works, the purpose of this paper is to establish a “warehouse shelfrelated” and scalable pick-by-AR systematic solution to improve the order picking performance within the actual workplace.
3 Methodology
3.1 Problem description
The physical setup of the order picking usually consists of storage racks, mobile carts, and components, which are designed together by logistics engineers. As shown in Fig. 1, it is a typical warehouse floor for our research within the automotive assembly line. The physical layout involved a picking aisle and storages on both sides, such as the fixed shelf and the mobile cart. While each fixed shelf contained 12 bins, the operator should pick a component from a fixed shelf which contains 12 storage bins labeled by Ai, Bi, ⋯, Li at the first section. And then, according to the predefined orders, the picked component should be placed to one of the 9 corresponding storage bins ai, bi, ⋯, ii in the mobile cart. After collecting enough components to the mobile cart, it would be translated to the next assembly step.
According to the order picking problem described above, if the worker wants to draw a long-term and correct picking performance within this high-density picking environment, strong mental efforts are indispensable to ensure the correct picking correspondence in each picking cycle, let alone the heavy-duty and time-starved components to be picked are in the automobile assembly line. Therefore, a friendly and intelligent method is expected to enable the order picking assistance, guiding the picker which one of the boxes to pick from and to place intuitively.
