In order to register visual instructions with actual workplace by pick-by-AR, the navigation for wearable AR devices in the warehouse floor is based on the proposed marker-based map. Therefore, besides the mapping accuracy for the warehouse floor, the navigation performance is another essential component for reliable wearable AR instruction on the warehouse floor.
To characterize the tracking performance based on the warehouse map, a single tag was placed in the scene while varying the distance. Given the marker size with 3.6 cm × 3.6 cm, sequential images (1280 × 720 pixels) are collected from the video camera to demonstrate the detection success rates of a fiducial tag at different distances (as shown in Fig. 8a). We can see that the fiducial marker can be recognized reliably when the measurement distance is below 200 cm. In addition, lots of real images are collected with a tag and two tags at an increasing distance. At each specific distance, the camera within the wearable AR device is aligned with the

center of a tag or the center of two tags, and then moved away from the marker plane perpendicularly. The ground truth distance is acquired by a laser tape, where the estimated distance for the camera to the tag is depicted in Fig. 8a, the results show that the tracking accuracy is believable when one marker is visible within 200 cm, and two markers would increase the effective perceived distance to about 250 cm. The results illustrate that valid tracking distance for pick-by-AR is increased when more markers are visible by the wearable AR device. The reason is that the global marker-based warehouse map is established beforehand in this study, enabling a more flexible and robust tracking for AR-based order picking. The results demonstrate that the pick-by-AR operations have better to perform within 200 cm away from the marker stuck on the shelf. Fortunately, almost all the manual picking operations are performed below 100 cm (around the length of an arm) in front of the warehouse shelf. Given the marker distribution on the warehouse shelf and the field of view of the camera in the wearable AR device, more than one marker is visible when the picker performs the picking performance, which enables a more robust localization ability for the proposed pick-byAR. Besides, the findings derived from statistical analyses also provide evidence for the designer to improve a more human-centered layout of the warehouse floor.
4.2 Improving order picking with wearable AR
Experiments are designed and carried out on an automotive assembly line (Fig. 9a). The proposed pick-by-AR method in the study is based on the wearable Seengene XMAN Glass (www.seengene.com), which has a wider field of view than the classical Google Glass and can convey the picking information to visual instructions via the head-mounted display. It is lightweight (about 33 g) enough to the picker, and the video camera equipped within the wearable glass can capture the image of the warehouse floor. The scenario consists of 6 fixed shelves and 2 mobile carts, and each rack owns about 8–12 compartments to store parts. All compartments have a unique ID consisting of letters and numbers. Fiducial markers are placed around each compartment, and the logical connection between the existed compartments and the unique marker ID is established in advance by the logistics designer.
Some images about the marker-based warehouse floor are captured by the camera in the smartphone (Fig. 9b). And then, with the proposed global mapping related to the fiducial markers in the warehouse floor, unique fiducial markers within the collected images can be extracted, matched, and reconstructed. The marker-based map about the fixed shelves and mobile carts with their compartments is established (Fig. 9c), providing robust global navigation for the subsequent pickby-AR application on the warehouse floor. Collected images with a higher resolution would result in a more accurate 3D marker-based map about the warehouse floor.

