5 Discussion
In this study, based on the global mapping of the markerbased warehouse floor, robust navigation related to the actual workplace for the wearable AR device is achieved, resulting in an efficient and error-avoid manual order picking performance. Fewer picking errors are made with pick-by-AR than with pick-by-paper, and several operators state that the picking information is easier to take in with the wearable AR device compared with the paper list. It also indicates that the proposed pick-by-AR system holds the potential for supporting a high-efficiency performance than pick-by-paper. The primary reason may be that the picking process and the picking target are displayed intuitively to the picker; thus, workers can perform the picking operation without doing mental efforts.
In pick-by-AR operations, Google Glass is used to support order picking with visual guidance for picking action displayed on the screen [21]. However, due to the lack of accurate navigation capacity in the warehouse floor, it is difficult to superimpose virtual instructions on the actual warehouse floor, exerting mental effort for the picker to find the target shelf bin in the actual workplace. Therefore, besides the study in terms of time efficiency and picking accuracy, robust and long-term navigation for the pick-by-AR application in the warehouse floor is still a key bottleneck. It can also be stated that the navigation system is the most problematic component for a wearable AR application, especially in an industrial environment [24].

