Recently, some dedicated equipment empowers the tracking ability for pick-by-AR. In pick-by-vision [12], the ART Dtrack (www.ar-tracking.de) was used to deliver a continuous pose estimation for wearable AR, but the user usually encountered the limits of the tracking capacity or occlusion. Besides, this tracking system is difficult to perform an extensive deployment due to its high price and poor adaptability. Microsoft Hololens is another representative highly integrated wearable AR device, and it can provide self-contained tracking performance for pick-by-AR. Nevertheless, the reference coordinate system of its tracking performance is established randomly with different pickers, and the tracking drift would accumulate during the order picking process, resulting in an incorrect visual instruction related to the actual shelf bins. Also, the uncomfortable weight of Microsoft Hololens prevents a long-term pick-by-AR application for workers. Thus, a warehouse shelf-related navigation method for pick-by-AR in an industrial environment is expected, and the marker-based tracking for pick-by-AR has comparative advantages. It is a low-cost and convenient style to deploy enough markers for long-term AR tracking on the warehouse floor. Krajcovic et al. [29] applied the fiducial marker to enable the tracking ability for pick-by-AR, but the tracking performance is derived from the individual target marker alone in the warehouse shelf. Due to the lack of an overall map about all the markers distributed on the warehouse floor, this pick-by-AR application is only available with the target marker in the field of view.
Therefore, a scalable wearable pick-by-AR system by empowering global navigation is carried out in this study. With lots of fiducial printed markers placed on the shelves, the overall marker-based map about the warehouse floor can be achieved. During the pick-by-AR on the warehouse floor, the global map empowers the navigation ability for the wearable AR device when one or more markers in the field of view. If the marker is associated with the in-progress component, the visual instruction is superimposed to an actual scene exactly to guide the order picking with the exact localization, quantity, and flow information. Even the marker in field of view is not the target one, visual guidance is also generated to assist the target shelf bins with the help of the global warehouse floor map. Also, more accurate navigation for pick-by-AR can be achieved when two or more markers visual in the field of view, and this warehouse shelfrelated tracking method for AR is immune to tracking drift.

In industrial applications, the choice of system to convey picking information to a friendly one should be proceeded by comprehensive analyses. Aside from the pick-by-AR with the head-up display, some other common options which include pick-by-paper, pick-by-light, and pick-by-voice are usually applied [16]. Besides the terms of accuracy, efficiency, and flexibility of the potential supporting system, the investment cost and ease of maintenance need also need to be taken into consideration. Moreover, this study also shows that the actual application of augmented reality on the shop floor is related to the complexity of the task and the experience of the worker. The proposed scalable and long-term pick-by-AR method in the automobile assembly line has shown a relative comparative advantage to some extent in all these aspects.
Besides, the systematic analyses about the picker performance in the pick-by-AR process are also of great significance, such as the research about potential ergonomics and human behavior for the operator during the picking performance [1], which are also our future work to improve the human-centered warehouse floor along with the pick-by-AR. Besides, although the proposed method can provide superimposed visual instructions on the actual scene for the worker, it is still hard to guarantee that all the picked components are the target ones as expected. Thus, based on the video camera in the AR glass applied in the paper, it is interesting to do further research about high-speed visual recognition for the component to confirm the pick operations. This recognition method has the potential to ensure the correctness of the picked component, reducing the incorrect picking errors furtherly. And we will try to do some researches about this and integrate it into the current head-mounted display, extending the current pick-by-AR applications.
6 Conclusion
The goal of this work is to develop a scalable and long-term wearable AR solution with reasonable investment costs, while supporting the order picking in a complex pick environment. Instead of the existed pick-by-AR applications, an easy-to-use global marker-based map about the warehouse floor is established, relating robust navigation in the warehouse floor for the pick-by-AR. It can establish a systematic spatial

