distribution about the storage places of items, resulting in a global visual instruction on a head-mounted display. Then, the proposed pick-by-AR method is integrated into a lightweight wearable AR device and evaluated in the actual warehouse workplace. Compared with the paper-based pick list, experimental results indicate that order picking with the support of AR can shorten the time of finding and identification of items, reducing the mental burden of workers in the process of picking selection, and a superior picking performance concerning the accuracy, speed, and usability is approached. We affirmed that the graphical pick list works better than the textual representation, and the conclusion is verified by the experiments in the automobile assembly line. Nevertheless, the problems of the AR applications in the picking process require further research especially in terms of closer integration of global map and warehouse management system, increasing the processability of linking the order picking system with an automatic identification system.
